Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [this fleet's riscv64 silicon implements RVV 0.7.1, not 1.0]

Real riscv64 CI (run 44206575156, job 135305237854): "Build wheel" succeeded after
~7h45m, then "Test triton wheel" ran a plain @triton.jit elementwise add kernel on
real torch tensors and died with `Illegal instruction`, exit code 132 (128+4 =
SIGILL), right after the one UserWarning print and before the kernel's own print -
i.e. inside the JIT-compiled kernel itself, on first execution, every time.

backend/riscv.py's DEFAULT_LLC_FEATURES hardcodes
"+m,+a,+f,+d,+c,+v,+zfh,+zvfh,+zba,+zbb,+zvfbfmin" (RVV + half-float-vector +
bitmanip) and backend/compiler.py's native (non-cross-compile) `_llir_to_bin`
codegen path passes that string to `llc -mattr=...` unconditionally - with no
runtime feature probe at all, i.e. gotcha 139's "compiled with RVV, no runtime
check at all" shape, except reached by a hardcoded default rather than a
build-time autodetect. The test kernel's `tl.load`/`+`/`tl.store` over
BLOCK_SIZE=1024-wide tiles lowers through MLIR's vector dialect, and with `+v`
set, `llc` is free to codegen that as real RVV (`vsetvli`, `vle32.v`, `vfadd.vv`,
`vse32.v` - see triton-riscv's own docs/06-RISCV-QEMU.md, which documents exactly
this instruction shape as the intended output of this path).

This repo's own CI fleet is already established, independently and repeatedly,
to be T-Head-class RVV-0.7.1 silicon that still advertises HWCAP_V (gotcha 272,
confirmed again for this same `ubuntu-24.04-riscv` runner pool by
gotcha 448's openvino ELF-attribute/opcode analysis: "the fleet is T-Head
(C906/C910/C920 class: TH1520, SG2042)... hence RVV 0.7.1... HWCAP advertises V;
the first RVV-1.0 `vsetvli` is still illegal"). LLVM's `llc` only ever emits the
RVV-1.0 vsetvli/vsetivli encoding (RVV 0.7.1 support was removed upstream years
ago), so any kernel whose IR actually contains a vector op and is codegen'd with
`+v` on this fleet hits exactly the symptom above: the very first `vsetvli`
traps. Gotcha 272 also already establishes QEMU cannot be used to confirm or
rule this out - TCG implements `vsetvli` correctly regardless of version, so
triton-riscv's own QEMU-based testing (docs/06-RISCV-QEMU.md,
scripts/triton-riscv-env.sh) would never have caught this; it only reproduces on
the real runner, which is what just happened.

Fix: lower DEFAULT_MARCH/DEFAULT_LLC_FEATURES to the plain rv64gc baseline
(I+M+A+F+D+C, matching this repo's established "RISCV64_GENERIC" safe baseline
for every other RVV-bearing port - see gotcha 448's "Pick RISCV64_GENERIC, not a
vector target" and gotcha 139's "capping the baseline to rv64gcv would make
every wheel require RVV hardware" framing, here with RVV-1.0-vs-0.7.1 added on
top of "require RVV at all"). +f,+d stay (lp64d is the real ABI; dropping them
causes soft-float link errors per the existing comment two lines above this
hunk) - only +v and its dependents (+zfh,+zvfh,+zvfbfmin) and the two bitmanip
extensions (+zba,+zbb, unverified on this fleet and not needed by anything this
backend currently emits) are dropped.

Also fix the native `_llir_to_bin` codegen branch to read the toolchain's
`llc_features` (TRITON_RISCV_LLC_FEATURES env var, falling back to the now-safe
DEFAULT_LLC_FEATURES) instead of the bare DEFAULT_LLC_FEATURES module constant
it had inlined directly. TRITON_RISCV_CROSS_COMPILE=1's codegen path already
reads this env var via RiscvToolchain.from_env(); the plain native
`@triton.jit` path - the one this wheel's own test, and every consumer's normal
import, actually exercises - silently did not, so the override this project
already documents (TRITON_RISCV_LLC_FEATURES) had no effect for a native run.
This keeps the safe default closed by this patch, while letting anyone who has
confirmed their own riscv64 target genuinely implements RVV 1.0 opt back in
without needing a repatch.

--- a/backend/riscv.py
+++ b/backend/riscv.py
@@ -11,9 +11,17 @@ import subprocess
 from typing import Iterable, Sequence

 DEFAULT_TRIPLE = "riscv64-unknown-linux-gnu"
-DEFAULT_MARCH = "rv64gcv_zfh_zvfh_zba_zbb"
+# Baseline is rv64gc (I+M+A+F+D+C), not the RVV/Zfh/Zba/Zbb superset upstream
+# defaults to. The real silicon this is JIT-compiled and executed on natively
+# can advertise HWCAP_V while actually implementing RVV 0.7.1, so any RVV-1.0
+# `vsetvli` llc emits for the superset SIGILLs at kernel-launch time; see the
+# patch header for the real-hardware evidence. TRITON_RISCV_MARCH/
+# TRITON_RISCV_LLC_FEATURES remain the override for anyone who has confirmed
+# their target genuinely implements RVV 1.0 (or the bitmanip/half-float
+# extensions) and wants them back.
+DEFAULT_MARCH = "rv64gc"
 DEFAULT_MABI = "lp64d"
-DEFAULT_LLC_FEATURES = "+m,+a,+f,+d,+c,+v,+zfh,+zvfh,+zba,+zbb,+zvfbfmin"
+DEFAULT_LLC_FEATURES = "+m,+a,+f,+d,+c"


 def _find_tool(
--- a/backend/compiler.py
+++ b/backend/compiler.py
@@ -835,13 +835,21 @@ def _llir_to_bin(llir: str, metadata, options=None):
                 # of generated Triton IR when optimized (including masked
                 # copies and scalar random-number kernels). Keep native
                 # codegen unoptimized until those backend issues are fixed.
+                #
+                # Read TRITON_RISCV_LLC_FEATURES here too (RiscvToolchain.
+                # from_env() falls back to DEFAULT_LLC_FEATURES otherwise):
+                # this native-run path previously hardcoded the module
+                # constant directly, so the env var that TRITON_RISCV_CROSS_
+                # COMPILE=1 already honors silently had no effect on a plain
+                # native `@triton.jit` call - the actual path a wheel's
+                # consumer exercises.
                 llc_args = [
                     llc_path,
                     src_path,
                     "-filetype=obj",
                     "-O0",
                     "-relocation-model=pic",
-                    f"-mattr={DEFAULT_LLC_FEATURES}",
+                    f"-mattr={RiscvToolchain.from_env().llc_features}",
                     "-o",
                     dst_path,
                 ]
